  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1122 AM EDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nWill focus on the near term for this discussion with a rather \nbusy morning with the continuation of convective activity along\na boundary draped from the west out over northern IL and then \nin a southeasterly orientation into our southern portions of the\nCWA. The activity has currently been developing along this \nboundary and training over the same areas which has increased \nthe threat of flash flooding and areal flooding in those areas. \nThis will only be exasperated further with the expectation of \nthe storms continuing across that area into the this evening \nwith periods of heavy rainfall to continue. A Flash Flood Watch \nis in effect until 8 PM EDT for all of the Indiana and Ohio \ncounties in our CWA. Also, with a large convective bow structure\nsitting northern IL and pushing eastward into the area this \nwill help push convection further north and southward from the \nboundary expanding the areal coverage to the rest of the CWA \ninto this late afternoon and early evening. \nShowers/thunderstorms may continue beyond that especially the \nsoutherly half of the CWA into the overnight hours. \n\nWith the very moist airmass in place and plenty of instability\nranging from around 1500 J/kg to the eastern portions of the\narea to around 4500 J/kg for our west and southwest portions of\nthe area. Bulk shear values of 40 to 50 kts with even a few\npockets around 60 kts pushing into the area from the west with\nthe MCS disturbance currently over northern IL. The increased\nshear looks to keep the things more organized and will be\ninteresting to see how this evolves as it pushes through the\narea. All threats are on the table but damaging winds and the\nheavy rainfall seems to be the main expected threats at this \ntime, but with marginal SR Helicity values around 100 m2/s2\ntornadic spin-ups would be possible especially along the main\nbowing structure and potential bookend vorticies. The Helicity\nvalues do look to peak later this afternoon in the rear of this\nbowing structure so will need to watch out for storms that may\ndevelop in it's wake later today for the potential for spin-ups.\nSPC does currently have a majority of our CWA, especially the\nsouthwestern half under an enhanced risk for severe storms\ntoday and a severe thunderstorm watch has been issued for the\nentire CWA until 5 PM EDT this evening.\n\nThe main boundary pushes southward tonight and will see the\nshower/thunderstorm activity do the same with the better chances\nfor mainly our southern portions of the CWA (mainly south of \nUS-30 and most likely south of US-24). SPC does have a marginal\nrisk for severe storms for our far southern portions of the CWA. \n\nWith this stagnant ring of fire/zonal flow pattern to continue \nthrough the end of the week and into the weekend periods of \nshowers/storms will be possible each day across the area with\nafternoons and evening periods with the greater chances.\nSeasonable temperatures will also remain the theme. However, will\nbe watching for ridging from the south to push northward which \nwill make for warmer temperatures and more humid conditions but\nstill a decent amount of uncertainty on the longer range\nforecast.",
